      description: "Antifungal drugs exploit structures or enzymes that differ from human cells, but each class reaches a different subset of fungi and body sites. Polyenes such as amphotericin B and nystatin bind ergosterol already present in the fungal membrane and create permeability defects. Azoles inhibit lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase, encoded by fungal CYP51/ERG11, so ergosterol falls and abnormal methylated sterols accumulate. Echinocandins inhibit Fks-containing beta-(1,3)-D-glucan synthase, weakening a cell-wall polymer humans do not make. Flucytosine is converted inside susceptible fungi into fluorinated metabolites that disrupt RNA and thymidylate synthesis. Terbinafine blocks squalene epoxidase earlier in ergosterol synthesis, while griseofulvin disrupts fungal mitosis and concentrates in newly formed keratin. These mechanistic differences explain why an echinocandin can be excellent for candidemia yet inactive against Cryptococcus, why nystatin treats local Candida but not bloodstream infection, and why a drug active in vitro may still fail if it cannot reach the infected compartment.",
