      description: "The systemic triazole comparison map distinguishes fluconazole, itraconazole, voriconazole, posaconazole, and isavuconazole by fungal spectrum, formulation, tissue distribution, metabolism, QT effect, interactions, and need for drug-level monitoring. All inhibit fungal lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51), reduce ergosterol synthesis, and impair membrane function, but organism susceptibility and human pharmacokinetics make them clinically noninterchangeable. Fluconazole is highly bioavailable, water soluble, renally cleared, and penetrates urine and cerebrospinal fluid well; it is useful for susceptible Candida and cryptococcal consolidation but has no reliable Aspergillus or Mucorales activity. Itraconazole covers several endemic fungi and Aspergillus but has variable absorption, major CYP3A interactions, poor CSF exposure, and a heart-failure warning. Voriconazole is a principal treatment for invasive Aspergillus and penetrates CNS, but CYP2C19 variability, nonlinear adult pharmacokinetics, visual effects, hepatotoxicity, photosensitivity, and interactions make exposure monitoring valuable. Posaconazole covers Candida, Aspergillus, and Mucorales; its delayed-release tablet, suspension, powder, and IV formulations cannot be casually substituted. Isavuconazonium is a water-soluble prodrug of isavuconazole for invasive aspergillosis and mucormycosis; it shortens QT and has fewer formulation barriers than voriconazole, but still has CYP3A interactions and hepatic risk.",
