      description: "Echinocandins are IV lipopeptides that inhibit the Fks catalytic subunits of beta-(1,3)-D-glucan synthase. Glucan is a load-bearing fungal cell-wall polymer absent from mammalian cells, so its loss weakens the wall and causes osmotic instability. They are fungicidal against most Candida and inhibit active Aspergillus hyphal growth, but they do not reliably treat Cryptococcus, Mucorales, or endemic dimorphic fungi and are not primary monotherapy for invasive aspergillosis. Caspofungin uses a loading dose and has hepatic dose considerations; micafungin does not require renal adjustment and is used for candidemia and selected Candida prophylaxis; anidulafungin undergoes slow nonenzymatic degradation; rezafungin has a prolonged half-life enabling weekly dosing for its labeled indication.",
      mechanism: "Fks1/Fks2 sit in the fungal plasma membrane and extrude newly synthesized glucan into the wall. Echinocandin binding lowers glucan, exposing cells to osmotic rupture and compensatory chitin remodeling. Mutations in FKS hot-spot regions reduce drug binding and raise MICs; prior echinocandin exposure and species such as Candida glabrata make this concern more important. Candida parapsilosis naturally has higher echinocandin MICs, which does not automatically prove clinical failure but makes species and susceptibility meaningful. Because echinocandins are large, highly protein-bound molecules with poor urinary and CSF penetration, a bloodstream-active drug may still be the wrong choice for Candida cystitis, prostatitis, endophthalmitis, or meningitis.",
