      description: "Candidemia is Candida recovered from blood and should be treated as invasive infection, not dismissed as contamination. An echinocandin is recommended initial therapy for most nonneutropenic adults because it is rapidly active against common Candida species and remains useful when fluconazole resistance is possible. Fluconazole can be initial therapy in selected stable patients unlikely to harbor a resistant species. The drug is only one part of cure: obtain follow-up blood cultures, remove or control infected catheters and other sources when appropriate, and evaluate persistent symptoms or cultures for endocarditis, endophthalmitis, thrombophlebitis, abscess, osteomyelitis, or another metastatic focus. Uncomplicated candidemia is generally treated for at least 14 days after the first documented negative blood culture and symptom resolution, not 14 days from the first dose.",
      mechanism: "Why start an echinocandin in most patients? It rapidly weakens Candida cell walls while species and azole susceptibility are still unknown, reducing the chance that initial fluconazole resistance leaves the bloodstream untreated. Why repeat blood cultures? They identify the first clearance day, expose persistent seeding, and define when an uncomplicated-course clock can begin. Why remove an implicated catheter or drain a collection? A colonized surface or undrained focus continuously releases organisms, so more drug cannot sterilize blood durably while the source remains. Why wait to step down? Oral fluconazole usually becomes reasonable after about 5-7 days only when clinical stability shows immediate control, negative cultures show bloodstream clearance, and susceptibility predicts continued activity. Why count at least 14 days from clearance and symptom resolution? Organisms deposited before clearance may remain in tissue after the first dose, so counting from admission would shorten effective treatment in a patient whose blood stayed positive. Why search for metastatic foci? Endocarditis, endophthalmitis, thrombophlebitis, abscess, bone infection, or implanted hardware changes penetration, source control, and duration. Neutropenia, species, organ involvement, and deep foci therefore make the pathway longer and more specialized.",
