      interactions: ["Strong CYP3A inhibitors increase isavuconazole; strong inducers reduce it and are contraindicated. Isavuconazole can raise tacrolimus, sirolimus, cyclosporine, and other CYP3A substrate exposure.", "Ritonavir exposure and effect are dose dependent; use the current label or interaction service rather than a class shortcut."],
      keyLabs: ["AST/ALT/alkaline phosphatase/bilirubin, potassium/magnesium, ECG in a short-QT or complex arrhythmia context, immunosuppressant levels, and cultures/imaging for response.", "Routine levels are not universally required, but a correctly timed level may help with unusual failure, toxicity, adherence, or interaction."],
      nclexTraps: ["Isavuconazonium is the prodrug; isavuconazole is active.", "It shortens QT and covers Mucorales, two distinctions from many other triazoles."],
      populationRisks: populationRisks("Current U.S. labeling includes IV use from age 1 year and capsules from age 6 years with minimum weight requirements and pediatric dosing tables. Do not extrapolate adult capsule dosing.", "Older adults need hepatic, QT history, interaction, and volume-status review, especially with transplant polypharmacy.", "May cause fetal harm based on animal data; use in pregnancy only when benefit justifies risk. Breastfeeding decisions require specialist review."),
      sourceNote: "Current U.S. Cresemba label: https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=8f7f73b8-586a-4df0-935f-fecd4696c16c",
