      usedToTreat: "Candidemia, acute disseminated candidiasis, Candida peritonitis/abscess, esophageal candidiasis, and prophylaxis of Candida infection in hematopoietic stem-cell transplant recipients under product-specific age and dose labeling.",
      description: "Micafungin is an IV echinocandin that inhibits beta-(1,3)-D-glucan synthesis, weakening a fungal cell-wall scaffold absent from human cells. It is fungicidal against many Candida species and useful as initial candidemia therapy while species and susceptibility are pending. It has no meaningful urinary or CSF concentration and does not treat Cryptococcus or Mucorales. Micafungin is highly protein bound, metabolized mainly through non-CYP pathways, and does not require renal or dialysis adjustment. Liver tests still matter, and prolonged high exposures produced hepatic foci and tumors in rats; human relevance is uncertain, so the label advises weighing benefit and hepatic risk rather than calling the drug liver safe.",
      mechanism: "Micafungin binds the Fks glucan-synthase complex, reducing wall glucan and causing osmotic instability. Mammalian cells lack the target, explaining relatively selective toxicity. FKS mutation can reduce susceptibility, and Candida parapsilosis naturally has higher MICs than many other Candida species. High protein binding limits glomerular filtration, while molecular size limits urine and CNS penetration. Histamine release during infusion can cause rash, pruritus, facial swelling, or vasodilation, which is why infusion rate and observation matter.",
      boxedWarning: noBoxed("Anaphylaxis, serious skin reactions, hemolysis/hemoglobinuria, renal effects, and hepatic injury can occur. Evaluate significant persistent liver abnormalities and use the lowest effective exposure and duration for the indication."),
