      nursingEssentials: ["Infuse over at least the label-specified time to reduce histamine reactions; never give as an IV push. Monitor line, skin, airway, blood pressure, and symptoms during administration.", "Trend liver tests, CBC/hemolysis clues, renal function, potassium, serial cultures, species/susceptibility, and source control. No renal adjustment does not mean no renal monitoring in a critically ill patient."],
      interactions: ["Micafungin can increase exposure to sirolimus, nifedipine, and itraconazole; monitor and reduce partner dose when toxicity appears under labeling.", "Its interaction burden is generally lower than systemic azoles, which can be useful in transplant and oncology patients while medication plans are stabilized."],
      keyLabs: ["AST/ALT/alkaline phosphatase/bilirubin, CBC/hemoglobin, creatinine, potassium, serial blood cultures, species/susceptibility, and focused source/deep-infection evaluation.", "Hemoglobin fall plus hemoglobinuria or hemolysis markers requires evaluation rather than being attributed automatically to critical illness."],
      nclexTraps: ["Micafungin has no renal dose adjustment but is not a urinary antifungal.", "A selective fungal-wall target lowers human toxicity; it does not eliminate hepatic, infusion, or hypersensitivity risk."],
      sourceNote: "Current U.S. micafungin labeling; mechanism reference: https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/getFile.cfm?setid=29f17423-5787-47d0-a83b-bbdc0198fbfa&type=pdf",
