      usedToTreat: "Topical dermatophyte and Candida skin infections, vaginal candidiasis with formulation-specific products, and treatment or prophylaxis of oropharyngeal candidiasis with slowly dissolved prescription troches.",
      description: "Clotrimazole is a mainly local imidazole antifungal that inhibits fungal lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51), reduces ergosterol, alters membrane permeability, and suppresses growth on skin, vaginal, or oral mucosa. A 10-mg troche is designed to dissolve slowly in the mouth, creating sustained salivary exposure for thrush; swallowing or chewing it defeats the dosage form. Topical cream treats susceptible dermatophytes and Candida at the skin surface but does not treat scalp, nail, bloodstream, or deep-organ infection simply because those conditions share a fungal name. Limited systemic absorption keeps interaction burden lower than systemic azoles, although troches can still affect liver tests and drug metabolism in selected patients.",
      mechanism: "CYP51 inhibition depletes ergosterol and accumulates abnormal sterols, disrupting membrane enzymes and permeability. At lower local concentrations clotrimazole is mainly fungistatic; higher concentrations can become fungicidal in vitro. The formulation creates the exposure: troche dissolution bathes oral mucosa, cream penetrates stratum corneum, and vaginal products concentrate locally. None reliably produces systemic blood levels for invasive disease. Symptom improvement can precede complete fungal clearance, explaining full-course adherence.",
      boxedWarning: noBoxed("Use the formulation only at its intended site. Persistent mouth lesions, dysphagia, fever, spreading rash, scalp/nail disease, pregnancy questions, or recurrent infection need diagnosis rather than indiscriminate over-the-counter retreatment."),
